# Every text file always checks out (and is stored) with LF line endings,
# regardless of the checking-out machine's own `core.autocrlf` setting -
# closes a real, confirmed bug, not a defensive guess: `windows-latest` CI
# (a fresh `actions/checkout@v4` clone, default `core.autocrlf=true`, no
# `.gitattributes` to override it) converted `push_stack_resource_seo.
# blade.xr`'s single trailing end-of-file newline from LF to CRLF on
# checkout. That byte isn't cosmetic - the view resolver's `@push`/
# `@endpush` handling only strips the *pushed* block itself, so whatever
# comes after `@endpush` (here, just that trailing newline) renders
# literally at the `<resource:...>` tag's own expansion site; `view_push_
# stack_resource.rs`'s own test asserts on that exact literal byte. A
# long-lived local working tree (checked out once, long before this file
# existed or before `core.autocrlf` was ever toggled) never re-normalizes
# on its own, so this stayed invisible on every developer machine and
# passed on `ubuntu-latest` besides - only a *fresh* Windows checkout
# actually corrupts the byte, which is exactly what CI does on every run.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary - `text=auto`'s own heuristic already skips these, but
# listed explicitly so a future file of the same type is never a question.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.sqlite binary
*.db binary
*.redb binary
